  title="How Long Rental Finishes Actually Last: Paint, Flooring, Counters, and Fixtures"
  description="Paint every three to five years, carpet three to seven, LVP ten to twenty. A rental finish lifespan list for Middle Tennessee owners, measured in turns."
>
<p>Rental finishes wear out on a schedule, and the schedule is shorter than the one in a house somebody owns. Interior paint in a rental generally needs a full repaint every three to five years, or about every other turn. Carpet lasts three to seven years depending on grade and traffic. Good luxury vinyl plank runs ten to twenty. Laminate countertops go ten to fifteen. Cabinet boxes outlive everything around them at fifteen to twenty five years, while the hinges and drawer slides inside them fail much sooner. Blinds are the shortest lived item in the unit, often one to three years. Below is the whole list, plus how to turn it into a plan instead of a surprise.</p>

<h2>Count turns, not years</h2>

<p>Two identical units can be five years old and in completely different shape. One had a single tenant with no pets who stayed five years. The other had three tenants, a dog, and two turns. Finishes wear from move ins, move outs, and traffic, not from the calendar.</p>

<p>So track both numbers for every unit. Years since install, and turns since install. The second one tells you more. A practical rule for most Middle Tennessee rentals: expect to make a real finish decision at every second turn, and expect walls, blinds, and caulk to need attention at every single one.</p>

<h2>Paint</h2>

<p>A full interior repaint every three to five years, or every other turn, is the pattern that holds up. Between repaints you are doing spot touch ups, which work only when the sheen and color still match. That is the case for keeping one standard color and one standard sheen across your entire portfolio, because the day you lose the formula is the day every touch up becomes a whole wall.</p>

<p>Sheen is the lifespan decision most owners get wrong. Flat hides drywall imperfections and cannot be washed, so hallways and kids' rooms end up with permanent marks. A washable eggshell or satin on walls, semi gloss on trim and doors, and a scrubbable product in kitchens and baths buys you years of touch ups instead of repaints.</p>

<p>Ceilings run longer, five to eight years, unless there is a smoker, a kitchen with no working range hood, or a past leak. Trim and doors hold up until the chips accumulate, usually in the five to eight year range.</p>

<h2>Flooring</h2>

<p><strong>Carpet:</strong> three to seven years in a rental. Grade and pad matter more than brand. Cheap carpet over cheap pad flattens in traffic lanes within two years, and then you are replacing it on the next turn anyway. Carpet in bedrooms only, with hard surface everywhere else, is the configuration that lasts.</p>

<p><strong>Luxury vinyl plank:</strong> ten to twenty years, and the wear layer tells you which end you are on. Wear layer is measured in mils. Six mil is a light residential product that will not survive a rental. Twelve mil holds up to normal tenant traffic. Twenty mil is commercial grade and shrugs off dogs and furniture drags. The difference in installed cost between six and twenty is real but small compared to replacing a floor two turns early.</p>

<p><strong>Sheet vinyl and vinyl composition tile:</strong> five to ten years, and the failure is almost always at the seams and edges rather than the field.</p>

<p><strong>Laminate:</strong> eight to fifteen years, with one caveat that decides it. Laminate swells permanently at any water event, so it does not belong in kitchens, baths, or laundry rooms in a rental.</p>

<p><strong>Tile:</strong> twenty years and up on the tile itself. Grout and caulk are the maintenance items, not the tile, and they want attention every few years.</p>

<p>One warning on partial replacement. Replacing flooring in one room almost never matches, even with the same product, because dye lots and production runs change. Plan flooring by area, hallway and living space together, or do not plan it at all.</p>

<h2>Counters, cabinets, and hardware</h2>

<p>Laminate counters give you ten to fifteen years, and they fail at the seams, at the sink cutout, and wherever a tenant set down a hot pan. Solid surface and quartz outlast the rest of the kitchen, which makes them worth considering only where the rent supports it. Our piece on <a href="/articles/what-upgrades-actually-raise-rent-middle-tennessee/">what upgrades actually raise rent in Middle Tennessee</a> covers where that line sits.</p>

<p>Cabinet boxes last fifteen to twenty five years if they stay dry. The parts that fail first are the moving parts. Hinges loosen and sag in five to ten years, drawer slides give out on a similar schedule, and both are cheap to replace while the boxes are still sound. The under sink cabinet floor is its own category, because a slow supply leak destroys particleboard long before anything else in the kitchen is worn out.</p>

<p>That sequence is why a cabinet decision is usually a doors and hardware decision, not a full replacement. Paint or reface the doors, replace the hinges and slides, replace the under sink base if it is soft, and keep the boxes.</p>

<h2>Plumbing fixtures and trim</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Faucets:</strong> ten to fifteen years for a decent one, and the cartridge inside is usually the actual repair.</li>
<li><strong>Braided supply lines:</strong> five to eight years. These are inexpensive and they are the single best thing to replace during a turn while the unit is empty.</li>
<li><strong>Angle stops and shutoff valves:</strong> ten to twenty years, and they fail when somebody needs them to work, which is why a turn is the time to check that each one actually closes.</li>
<li><strong>Toilet flappers:</strong> two to four years. Fill valves, four to six. A running toilet is a water bill problem long before it is a plumbing problem.</li>
<li><strong>Wax rings:</strong> replace whenever the toilet is pulled, every time, no exceptions.</li>
<li><strong>Shower and tub caulk:</strong> every one to three years, and it is the cheapest leak prevention in the building.</li>
<li><strong>Garbage disposals:</strong> five to ten years in a rental, often shorter, because tenants put things in them.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The short list, the stuff that goes every turn</h2>

<p>Blinds, one to three years. Door stops and bumpers. Cabinet and closet door hardware. Toilet seats. Shower heads. Drip pans on electric ranges. Range hood filters. Dryer vent cleaning. Window and door screens. Smoke and carbon monoxide units on their own replacement schedule, which is set by the manufacturer's date rather than by your turn cycle. These are all small, and together they are most of what a tenant notices in the first week.</p>

<h2>Systems and appliances have their own clocks</h2>

<p>Finishes are a different budget line from systems. We covered the mechanical side in <a href="/articles/how-long-do-home-systems-last/">how long home systems last</a>, and the appliance replacement schedule in <a href="/articles/rental-appliance-replacement-cost/">rental appliance replacement</a>. Keep them separate when you plan, because systems fail on their own timing and finishes fail on your turn schedule.</p>

<h2>Middle Tennessee specifics</h2>

<p>Two local conditions shorten these numbers. Summer humidity and crawlspace moisture swell door bottoms, lift flooring edges, and raise the odds of a musty smell in a vacant unit, so vacant units want the air conditioning left running rather than shut off. And the clay soil here moves, which puts hairline cracks in drywall corners and above door frames on a cycle. Those cracks are usually cosmetic and they come back after they are patched, which is worth knowing before you spend a turn chasing them.</p>

<h2>Turning this into a plan</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Build a finish register per unit.</strong> One page. What was installed, when, what product and color, and how many turns ago. Keep paint codes and flooring product numbers on it.</li>
<li><strong>Decide at the turn, not at the complaint.</strong> An empty unit is the only cheap time to do flooring, paint, and anything that requires moving furniture.</li>
<li><strong>Replace by area, not by room.</strong> It is the only way the result matches.</li>
<li><strong>Batch like work across units.</strong> Four units repainted in one stretch move faster than four units repainted across four months.</li>
<li><strong>Fund it ahead of time.</strong> This is what a reserve is for, and we worked through the math in <a href="/articles/rental-maintenance-budget-how-much-to-set-aside/">how much to set aside for rental maintenance</a>.</li>
</ol>

<p>If you want a straight read on where a unit actually stands, we walk it with you before the tenant is out, note which finishes are at end of life and which have another turn left in them, and put the whole thing on one flat quote and one schedule. Ask for a walkthrough and you will have your numbers before the unit is vacant.</p>
